(vector (map (:adr/authoritative_for (vector "kotoba-lang/dtn の replay 対策（:dtn/sequence-number high-water mark、:replay-state-path 永続化）の設計根拠")), (:adr/supersedes (vector)), (:adr/superseded_by (vector)), (:adr/topic "telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite"), (:adr/related (vector "90-docs/adr/2607161743-kotoba-lang-rcs-dtn-independent-telecom-substrate.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607161817-kotoba-lang-dtn-tcp-transport-operational.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607161852-kotoba-lang-dtn-durable-store-bundle-integrity.edn" "90-docs/adr/2607161922-kotoba-lang-dtn-multi-hop-relay-routing.edn" "orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn")), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/id "adr-2607161951-kotoba-lang-dtn-replay-protection"), (:adr/doc_type :adr), (:adr/last_verified "2026-07-16"), (:adr/body "---\nid: adr-2607161951-kotoba-lang-dtn-replay-protection\ntitle: \"ADR-2607161951: kotoba-lang/dtn gains per-source sequence-number replay protection for authenticated peers\"\nstatus: accepted\ndoc_type: adr\ntopic: telecom-independent-substrate-dtn-rcs-mesh-satellite\nauthoritative: true\nlast_verified: 2026-07-16\nauthoritative_for:\n  - kotoba-lang/dtn の replay 対策（:dtn/sequence-number high-water mark、:replay-state-path 永続化）の設計根拠\nrelated:\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607161743-kotoba-lang-rcs-dtn-independent-telecom-substrate.edn\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607161817-kotoba-lang-dtn-tcp-transport-operational.edn\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607161852-kotoba-lang-dtn-durable-store-bundle-integrity.edn\n  - 90-docs/adr/2607161922-kotoba-lang-dtn-multi-hop-relay-routing.edn\n  - orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn\nsupersedes: []\nsuperseded_by: []\n---\n\n# ADR-2607161951: kotoba-lang/dtn gains per-source sequence-number replay protection for authenticated peers\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-16\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## Problem\n\nADR-2607161852's HMAC bundle-integrity work explicitly named, as a known\nlimitation, that it provided no replay protection: a legitimately-signed\nbundle captured off the wire could be re-sent later and would be accepted\nagain, since the signature alone says nothing about \"have I seen this\nexact bundle, or an older one from this source, before.\" This was the\nnext item from the gap inventory, closed here.\n\n## Decision\n\n### Decision 1: reuse the bundle's existing `:dtn/sequence-number` field rather than invent a new one\n\n`kotoba.dtn/bundle` (established back in ADR-2607161743) already carries a\n`:dtn/sequence-number` field, previously always left at its default (0) by\nevery caller. Rather than add a parallel nonce/timestamp scheme, this ADR\nputs that existing field to work: `kotoba.dtn.transport.tcp/send-message!`\nnow stamps each newly-originated bundle with the next value from a\nper-node monotonic counter before signing; a relaying node (from\nADR-2607161922's `:relay` action) leaves an in-transit bundle's sequence\nnumber untouched, since the relay isn't the bundle's source.\n\nThis composes for free with the existing HMAC signing: `sign-bundle`\nalready covers the whole bundle map, so `:dtn/sequence-number` is already\ntamper-protected wherever `:peer-secrets` is configured — an attacker\ncannot forge a higher sequence number without the shared secret, and\ncannot successfully replay an old captured (legitimately-signed) bundle if\nthe receiver tracks and enforces a per-source high-water mark.\n\n### Decision 2: replay protection is explicitly a property of authenticated peers only\n\n`kotoba.dtn.auth` gained pure functions `replay?` and\n`update-high-water-mark`; the transport layer applies them ONLY to bundles\nthat already passed HMAC verification for a `:peer-secrets`-configured\nsource. A bundle whose sequence number is not strictly greater than the\nhighest already accepted from that exact source is rejected — logged as\n`DTN-REPLAY-REJECTED`, dropped (not stored, not retried, not relayed\nfurther), the same \"security event, not a legitimate delivery\" treatment\nestablished for signature failures in ADR-2607161852. Unauthenticated\npeers (no `:peer-secrets` entry) are unaffected — tracking a claimed\nsequence number from an unauthenticated source would be meaningless, since\nan attacker could simply also forge a high number.\n\n### Decision 3: persist the high-water mark, not just document it as a gap\n\nThe design note in ADR-2607161852's own text raised the risk explicitly:\nan in-memory-only high-water mark resets to zero on restart, silently\nreopening the replay window for anything captured before that restart.\nRather than ship that as a documented limitation, this ADR closes it: an\noptional `:replay-state-path`, mirroring `kotoba.dtn.store`'s existing\n`:store-path` pattern exactly (pure serialize/deserialize functions +\nNode-only `node:fs` I/O behind the same portable-core split), persists the\nper-source high-water-mark map to disk on every accepted update and\nreloads it on `start-node!`. Verified with an actual simulated-restart\nround-trip (stop the node, start a fresh one against the same path,\nconfirm the reloaded high-water mark still rejects a pre-restart replay) —\nnot merely asserted to work.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `clojure -M:test` → 30 tests / 147 assertions (was 126; +21 new — router relay tests plus this round's auth/replay coverage), 0 failures, 0 errors. Independently re-run from a cold clone.\n- `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Independently re-run.\n- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../phone/src:../html/src:../css/src\" test/kotoba/dtn/transport/tcp_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 7/7 scenarios passed`, exit 0. Scenarios 1-5 (transport, durability, integrity, relay) from the three prior ADRs unmodified and still passing; new scenario 6 sends a legitimate message, replays the EXACT captured signed bundle over a raw socket (confirmed rejected — B's inbox count for that message stays at 1, `DTN-REPLAY-REJECTED` logged), then sends a genuinely new message with a fresh sequence number (confirmed accepted — replay rejection doesn't wrongly block subsequent legitimate traffic). Independently re-run from a cold clone, output matches the implementing agent's report.\n- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit `523ae481177ac3d2ed72156a76c24fdead49799f`.\n- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry dtn` (1-line diff, `54fe04c34eaa→523ae481177a`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching all prior ADRs in this series.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- A previously-explicit, named security gap (replay of captured\n  legitimately-signed bundles) is closed for authenticated peers, with the\n  fix surviving a process restart — not just an in-memory best-effort.\n- The fix required zero new wire-format fields; it activates an\n  already-existing, previously-inert part of the RFC 9171 primary-block\n  model (`:dtn/sequence-number`), which is a small but real confirmation\n  that ADR-2607161743's original bundle design had the right shape even\n  before every field in it was put to use.\n- Replay protection remains scoped to authenticated peers only — an\n  unauthenticated peer relationship has no replay protection, same as it\n  has no origin authentication, unchanged from ADR-2607161852.\n- Still explicitly undone, unchanged from the prior inventory: PKI\n  (pre-shared-secret HMAC remains the mechanism), automatic route\n  discovery, NAT traversal, remaining RFC 9171 wire-format details (CBOR,\n  BPSec, fragmentation), mesh-radio/satellite hardware, real carrier\n  gateway credentials, a consuming cloud-itonami actor, and — newly\n  identified while researching what would come next — genuine real-I/O\n  layers for `kotoba-lang/net` and `kotoba-lang/turn` (both currently\n  protocol-semantics-only, same as `kotoba-lang/dtn` was before\n  ADR-2607161817), which is why this specific ADR does not yet address\n  peer-discovery or NAT-traversal composition despite those being named as\n  intended future composition points since ADR-2607161743.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- **A separate nonce/timestamp-window scheme instead of the existing\n  sequence-number field.** Rejected: `:dtn/sequence-number` already\n  exists in the bundle model and is already covered by the HMAC signature;\n  inventing a parallel mechanism would add a wire-format field and\n  verification path with no benefit over using what was already there.\n- **Leaving the high-water mark in-memory-only, documented as a known\n  gap** (the option ADR-2607161852 explicitly left open as acceptable).\n  Rejected in favor of actually implementing persistence: the\n  `kotoba.dtn.store` pattern to mirror was already proven out, so the\n  marginal cost of doing it for real was low relative to the security\n  value of not silently reopening the replay window on every restart.\n- **Applying replay tracking to unauthenticated peers too** (tracking\n  sequence numbers regardless of signature status). Rejected: without a\n  real signature, a claimed sequence number carries no trust — tracking it\n  would be security theater, not protection.\n\n## References\n\n- `90-docs/adr/2607161743-kotoba-lang-rcs-dtn-independent-telecom-substrate.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607161817-kotoba-lang-dtn-tcp-transport-operational.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607161852-kotoba-lang-dtn-durable-store-bundle-integrity.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2607161922-kotoba-lang-dtn-multi-hop-relay-routing.edn`\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/README.edn` — https://github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn\n- `orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn/src/kotoba/dtn/auth.cljc`\n"), (:adr/artifacts "{:kotoba-lang-dtn \"kotoba-lang/dtn @ 523ae481177a (pushed, public)\", :manifest \"orgs/kotoba-lang/dtn pin advanced via --entry dtn, pin-verified\"}"), (:db/id -1), (:adr/authoritative true), (:adr/title "ADR-2607161951: kotoba-lang/dtn gains per-source sequence-number replay protection for authenticated peers"), (:adr/source-format "edn")))